<!--
  Purple Team Home Lab - Wazuh custom rules (v3)
  Full write-up: v3/README.md and detections/README.md

  Deploy:
      cp local_rules.xml /var/ossec/etc/rules/
      /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-analysisd -t     # must print no WARNING and no ERROR
      systemctl restart wazuh-manager

  Verified by targeted trigger on 24 Oct 2026 (times UTC):
      100200  11:42:39  level 12   powershell.exe OpenProcess(lsass, 0x1FFFFF)
      100201  11:54-58  0 alerts   20 platform EID 10 events passed through the chain
      100205  11:42:16  0 alerts   MsMpEng.exe read lsass at 0x1010
      100210  11:48:57  level 12   task with IEX + DownloadString
      100211  11:49:04  level 10   task with -nop -w hidden -enc
      negative tests: defrag task and a task running
      "powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File ..." both stayed at 60228 level 4.

  Three things that are easy to get wrong here, all found by testing:

  1. The EID 10 group is `sysmon_event_10`, with an underscore. `sysmon_event10` does not
     exist; a rule bound to it loads, reports as coverage and is discarded with
     WARNING (7610). Do not use `sysmon_eid10_detections` as a parent either - that group is
     carried only by events another rule already matched.

  2. Built-in 92900 filters on grantedAccess being 0x1010 or 0x40, so it is not a usable
     parent for a full-access mask. It is also why the default ruleset alerts at level 12 on
     Defender's routine read and stays silent on 0x1fffff and 0x1438.

  3. Wazuh writes Windows paths in eventdata with doubled backslashes. Patterns use \\+ so
     they match either form. Check with repr(), not print().

  Mask list is short on purpose. Measured against 935 EID 10 reads of lsass.exe sampled from
  THEPUNISHER: a draft that also matched 0x1010 and 0x1410 alerted 90 times, 89 of them
  platform behaviour (79 WmiPrvSE.exe at 0x1410; wazuh-agent.exe itself reads LSASS at
  0x1fffff). The shipped version alerts once on the same sample. 0x1010 and 0x1410 are left
  to v4 as a low-severity hunt tier.

  Coverage limit: Wazuh receives Sysmon from 1 of this lab's 3 Windows agents, so 100200 can
  only fire on THEPUNISHER. The 4698 rules are unaffected; the Security channel is read
  everywhere.
-->

<group name="windows,sysmon,credential_access,">

  <!--
    Dump-capable handle on LSASS.
      0x1438 / 0x143a       masks MiniDumpWriteDump commonly requests
      0x1f0fff - 0x1fffff   near-full / full access
    0x1f3fff is the mask recorded from notepad.exe during the v3 SharpKatz run
    (v3 detection screenshot 07).
  -->
  <rule id="100200" level="12">
    <if_group>sysmon_event_10</if_group>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\+lsass\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.grantedAccess" type="pcre2">(?i)^0x(1438|143a|1f0fff|1f1fff|1f2fff|1f3fff|1fffff)$</field>
    <description>Dump-capable handle opened on LSASS - possible credential dumping (T1003.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,pci_dss_10.6.1,gdpr_IV_35.7.d,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Platform processes observed reading LSASS on this baseline. Paths are anchored in full,
    so a payload named svchost.exe running from C:\Temp still alerts at level 12.

    Not covered: code injected into the real signed svchost.exe or MsMpEng.exe inherits a
    legitimate SourceImage and is suppressed. Separating that needs CallTrace (an LSASS read
    whose stack runs through UNKNOWN frames or a non-Microsoft module). Left out because this
    lab has no sample to test such a pattern against.

    100201 and 100205 overlap deliberately: 100201 guards the high-severity path, 100205
    guards built-in 92900 directly, so a benign platform read is suppressed either way.
  -->
  <rule id="100201" level="0">
    <if_sid>100200</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.sourceImage" type="pcre2">(?i)^C:\\+(Windows\\+System32\\+(svchost|MRT|taskmgr)\.exe|Windows\\+System32\\+wbem\\+WmiPrvSE\.exe|ProgramData\\+Microsoft\\+Windows Defender\\+Platform\\+[^\\]+\\+MsMpEng\.exe|Program Files\\+VMware\\+VMware Tools\\+vmtoolsd\.exe|Program Files \(x86\)\\+ossec-agent\\+wazuh-agent\.exe)$</field>
    <description>LSASS access by a known-benign platform process - suppressed</description>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Fixes a false positive present in the lab today, independent of 100200. Built-in 92900
    fires at level 12 for any LSASS read with read permissions. All 32 EID 10 alerts Wazuh
    has recorded here are benign: MsMpEng.exe (29), svchost.exe (2), MRT.exe (1).
  -->
  <rule id="100205" level="0">
    <if_sid>92900</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.sourceImage" type="pcre2">(?i)^C:\\+(Windows\\+System32\\+(svchost|MRT|taskmgr)\.exe|Windows\\+System32\\+wbem\\+WmiPrvSE\.exe|ProgramData\\+Microsoft\\+Windows Defender\\+Platform\\+[^\\]+\\+MsMpEng\.exe|Program Files\\+VMware\\+VMware Tools\\+vmtoolsd\.exe|Program Files \(x86\)\\+ossec-agent\\+wazuh-agent\.exe)$</field>
    <description>Routine platform LSASS read - suppresses the level-12 false positive from rule 92900</description>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<group name="windows,windows_security,persistence,">

  <!--
    Built-in 60228 fires at level 4 for every 4698, so a malicious task and a benign OneDrive
    update task score the same. These read the task definition and elevate the ones carrying
    a download cradle or an obfuscated launcher.

    taskContent arrives HTML-escaped (&lt;Arguments&gt;...), so the task XML's angle brackets
    are encoded while the command text inside is not. Patterns key on command tokens.
  -->
  <rule id="100210" level="12">
    <if_sid>60228</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.taskContent" type="pcre2">(?i)(IEX|Invoke-Expression|DownloadString|DownloadFile|Net\.WebClient|FromBase64String)</field>
    <description>Scheduled task created with a PowerShell download cradle - likely persistence (\Updater pattern)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,pci_dss_10.6.1,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100211" level="10">
    <if_sid>60228</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.taskContent" type="pcre2">(?i)(-enc\s|-EncodedCommand|-w\s+hidden|-WindowStyle\s+Hidden|-nop\b|-NoProfile)</field>
    <description>Scheduled task created with an obfuscated or hidden-window PowerShell launcher</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1053.005</id>
      <id>T1027</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>persistence,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
